# Cursor AI Rules for Sumbandila Project

## Project Context
This is the Sumbandila verification platform - a microservices-based application for verifying South African institutions and professionals.

## Code Style & Standards

### TypeScript/JavaScript
- Use TypeScript for all new code
- Enable strict mode
- No `any` types without justification
- Use functional components with hooks
- Prefer const over let
- Use async/await over promises
- Use descriptive variable names

### Python
- Follow PEP 8 style guide
- Use type hints for all functions
- Use Pydantic for data validation
- Use async/await for I/O operations
- Write docstrings for all public functions
- Use Black for formatting (line length: 100)

### General
- Write self-documenting code
- Add comments for complex logic
- Keep functions small and focused
- Follow SOLID principles
- Use meaningful commit messages (Conventional Commits)

## Architecture Patterns

### Microservices
- Each service has a single responsibility
- Services communicate via REST APIs
- Use event-driven architecture where appropriate
- Implement circuit breakers for resilience

### API Design
- Use RESTful conventions
- Version all APIs (/api/v1/)
- Return consistent response formats
- Include proper error handling
- Implement rate limiting

### Database
- Use migrations for schema changes
- Index frequently queried columns
- Use connection pooling
- Implement soft deletes where appropriate

## Security

### Authentication & Authorization
- Use JWT for authentication
- Implement RBAC for authorization
- Rotate secrets regularly
- Never commit secrets to Git

### Input Validation
- Validate all user input
- Sanitize data before database queries
- Use parameterized queries
- Implement request size limits

### API Security
- Use HTTPS in production
- Implement CORS properly
- Add security headers
- Rate limit public endpoints

## Testing

### Unit Tests
- Test business logic thoroughly
- Mock external dependencies
- Aim for 70%+ coverage
- Use descriptive test names

### Integration Tests
- Test service interactions
- Test database operations
- Test API endpoints
- Use test databases

### E2E Tests
- Test critical user flows
- Test on multiple browsers/devices
- Automate where possible

## Documentation

### Code Documentation
- Document public APIs with JSDoc/docstrings
- Explain "why" not "what" in comments
- Keep documentation up-to-date
- Include examples in documentation

### README Files
- Keep README files current
- Include setup instructions
- Document environment variables
- Add architecture diagrams

## Performance

### Frontend
- Lazy load components
- Optimize images
- Minimize bundle size
- Use code splitting
- Implement caching

### Backend
- Use database indexes
- Implement caching (Redis)
- Optimize database queries
- Use async operations
- Monitor performance metrics

## Deployment

### CI/CD
- Run tests on every push
- Lint and type-check code
- Build and verify artifacts
- Deploy to staging first
- Verify deployments

### Monitoring
- Log important events
- Track error rates
- Monitor response times
- Set up alerts
- Use structured logging

## AI Agent Development

### Prompt Engineering
- Be specific and clear
- Provide examples
- Define output format
- Set boundaries
- Include context

### Memory Management
- Implement short-term memory
- Use vector stores for long-term memory
- Manage context window
- Prune old memories

### Tool Integration
- Define clear tool interfaces
- Validate tool inputs
- Handle tool errors gracefully
- Log tool usage

## Git Workflow

### Branches
- main: production-ready code
- feature/*: new features
- fix/*: bug fixes
- refactor/*: code improvements
- docs/*: documentation updates

### Commits
- Use Conventional Commits format
- Write descriptive commit messages
- Keep commits atomic
- Reference issues in commits

### Pull Requests
- Write clear PR descriptions
- Link related issues
- Request reviews
- Ensure CI passes
- Update documentation

## File Organization

### Monorepo Structure
```
apps/       - Applications (web, mobile)
services/   - Backend services
packages/   - Shared code
docs/       - Documentation
scripts/    - Automation scripts
infrastructure/ - IaC and configs
```

### Service Structure
```
service/
├── app/
│   ├── api/        - API routes
│   ├── models/     - Data models
│   ├── schemas/    - Validation schemas
│   ├── services/   - Business logic
│   └── core/       - Core utilities
├── tests/          - Test files
├── main.py         - Entry point
└── requirements.txt - Dependencies
```

## Environment Variables

### Required Variables
- DATABASE_URL
- REDIS_URL
- JWT_SECRET
- OPENAI_API_KEY (for AI features)

### Optional Variables
- LOG_LEVEL (default: INFO)
- ENVIRONMENT (development/staging/production)
- SENTRY_DSN (for error tracking)

## Common Tasks

### Adding a New Feature
1. Create feature branch
2. Implement feature with tests
3. Update documentation
4. Create pull request
5. Get code review
6. Merge to main

### Fixing a Bug
1. Create fix branch
2. Write failing test
3. Fix the bug
4. Verify test passes
5. Create pull request

### Deploying
1. Merge to main
2. CI/CD runs tests
3. Build artifacts
4. Deploy to staging
5. Verify staging
6. Deploy to production
7. Monitor deployment

## Best Practices

### Do
- Write tests first (TDD)
- Keep functions small
- Use meaningful names
- Handle errors properly
- Log important events
- Document complex logic
- Review your own code first
- Ask for help when stuck

### Don't
- Commit secrets
- Skip tests
- Ignore linter warnings
- Leave TODO comments
- Copy-paste code
- Commit commented code
- Push directly to main
- Ignore security warnings

